Git commit - A commit is a recorded snapshot of changes in your Git history.

| Type | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `feat` | Introduces functionality | `feat(api): add employee endpoints` |
| `fix` | Fixes a defect | `fix(api): correct employee lookup` |
| `test` | Adds or updates tests | `test(api): test invalid employee data` |
| `docs` | Changes documentation | `docs(readme): add setup instructions` |
| `refactor` | Restructures code without intentionally changing behavior | `refactor(api): simplify employee queries` |
| `style` | Formatting changes without changing program logic | `style(api): format controller code` |
| `perf` | Improves performance | `perf(employee): eager load departments` |
| `chore` | Maintenance work | `chore(deps): update development dependencies` |

git remote -v  -- shows the configured remotes.

git push -u origin feature/employee-api 
git push: Upload local commits to a remote repository.
origin: The remote repository's configured name.
feature/employee-api: The branch to upload.
-u: Set the upstream relationship between your local branch and its remote counterpart.
git commit records your changes locally; git push sends your commits to the remote repository.

